WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:04.720
The MCU's storytelling is so generally&nbsp;
successful that you may have ignored&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:09.840
Tony Stark objectifying Black Widow.&nbsp;
Or the Hulk dabbing. Or the bodily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:14.400
fluids covering the Milano. Keep&nbsp;
watching for more MCU missteps.

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:20.560
Natasha Romanoff, aka the Black Widow, is one of&nbsp;
the most capable characters in the entire MCU.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:26.240
Which is just one reason why it's so cringeworthy&nbsp;
that her introduction revolves almost entirely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.720
around her being objectified by Tony&nbsp;
Stark, including a bunch of needlessly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:36.640
gratuitous lingerie modeling photos. Oh,&nbsp;
and maybe the worst line in the whole MCU.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:37.200
"I want one"

00:00:37.760 --> 00:00:43.840
"I want one." Outside of the gross, surface-level&nbsp;
objectification of those three words, it doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:48.880
help that his love interest Pepper Potts happens&nbsp;
to be the person to whom the line is delivered.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:52.480
Johansson herself acknowledged her&nbsp;
own role in how the sexualization&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.480 --> 00:00:57.440
of superheroes developed while doing press&nbsp;
in 2019 for "Black Widow." Needless to say,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.440 --> 00:01:01.040
her outlook on the character has&nbsp;
changed a lot over the last decade.

00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:06.960
"It's hard because I'm inside it, but probably&nbsp;
a lot of that is actually from me too.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.960 --> 00:01:12.320
I'll be 35 years old and I'm a mom and my&nbsp;
life is different. Obviously, 10 years have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:18.080
passed and things have happened and I have a much&nbsp;
different, more evolved understanding of myself."

00:01:18.080 --> 00:01:23.120
That's one big reason she wanted to return to&nbsp;
the MCU for a Black Widow solo film - to help&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:28.160
change the narrative around female superheroes&nbsp;
in general, and Black Widow specifically.

00:01:28.160 --> 00:01:34.000
"It's changing now. Now people, young girls,&nbsp;
are getting a much more positive message,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.000 --> 00:01:38.080
but it's been incredible to be a part&nbsp;
of that shift and be able to come out&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:42.320
the other side and be a part of&nbsp;
that old story, but also progress."

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:47.920
Of course, cringe-worthy quotes about Black&nbsp;
Widow aren't limited to MCU characters;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.920 --> 00:01:52.880
we've heard them from MCU actors as well.&nbsp;
While doing an interview with Digital Spy UK&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.880 --> 00:01:58.080
in advance of 2015's "Avengers: Age of Ultron,"&nbsp;
Captain America actor Chris Evans and Hawkeye&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:03.200
actor Jeremy Renner made some pretty tasteless&nbsp;
remarks about Scarlett Johansson's character.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:08.800
When Digital Spy asked them a question of dubious&nbsp;
seriousness about MCU fans shipping Black Widow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:14.160
with both Cap and Hawkeye, Renner decided&nbsp;
to go full locker room with his response.

00:02:14.160 --> 00:02:18.560
"She's a s—."

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:19.280
[laughing]

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:25.760
Though the remarks were apparently meant in&nbsp;
good humor, they came off completely tone deaf,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:29.360
especially considering complaints about the&nbsp;
depiction of the character to that point.

00:02:29.360 --> 00:02:33.360
Both actors faced an understandable amount&nbsp;
of backlash for their respective remarks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.360 --> 00:02:39.200
via social media and each issued a statement.&nbsp;
For his part, Evans gave a heartfelt apology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.240
saying that they, quote, "answered in a&nbsp;
very juvenile and offensive way." He added,

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.800
"I regret it and sincerely apologize."

00:02:47.440 --> 00:02:49.920
Renner, on the other hand, issued a classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.920 --> 00:02:54.880
passive aggressive non-apology which made&nbsp;
it clear he thought the backlash was dumb.

00:02:54.880 --> 00:02:58.960
"I am sorry that this tasteless joke about&nbsp;
a fictional character offended anyone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:01.120
It was not meant to be serious in any way."

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:04.880
"I was talking about a fictional character&nbsp;
and fictional behavior. But, Conan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:10.240
if you slept with four of the six Avengers, no&nbsp;
matter how much fun you had, you'd be a s—."

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:12.880
Still no apologies for his music career, however.

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:16.560
You'd think Marvel would have&nbsp;
learned from those controversies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:21.680
but guess which character they mishandled&nbsp;
yet again in "Avengers: Age of Ultron?"

00:03:21.680 --> 00:03:26.480
One of the least beloved plot developments in&nbsp;
the entire history of the MCU was the sudden,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.480 --> 00:03:30.320
weird romance between Bruce Banner&nbsp;
and Natasha Romanoff in "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.320 --> 00:03:34.400
Age of Ultron." While both characters&nbsp;
are each separately fan favorites,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:39.840
their relationship seemed ill-fitting, especially&nbsp;
given that their most memorable scene together up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.960
to that point was a sequence in Avengers where&nbsp;
Hulk chased Natasha around trying to murder her.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:49.360
But that uncomfortable fact paled in&nbsp;
comparison with the horribly tone-deaf&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:54.240
scene in Age of Ultron where Natasha tells&nbsp;
Bruce that she's a monster too because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:58.160
thanks to a forced hysterectomy,&nbsp;
she can no longer have children.

00:03:58.160 --> 00:04:00.240
"You still think you're the&nbsp;
only monster on the team?”

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:06.800
Needless to say, many fans and critics didn't&nbsp;
appreciate the idea that Natasha's value as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.400
human being extended only as far as her ability&nbsp;
to bear children. Marvel made a small attempt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:17.200
to address the controversy in the film "Black&nbsp;
Widow," but given the nature of the multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.200 --> 00:04:21.840
controversies "Age of Ultron" director Joss&nbsp;
Whedon has been in over the past few years,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:25.760
this scene has actually gotten even&nbsp;
worse with age, rather than better.

00:04:26.640 --> 00:04:31.680
Speaking of Bruce Banner, perhaps the MCU&nbsp;
went more than a little bit overboard in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.920
trying to convey what a dork he is.&nbsp;
Besides burdening him with tons of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.920 --> 00:04:40.080
nonsensical science jargon in multiple&nbsp;
films, Natasha Romanoff just straight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:43.440
up said it in "Age of Ultron," which&nbsp;
you'd think would have been enough.

00:04:43.440 --> 00:04:47.520
"He's also a huge dork. Chicks dig that!"

00:04:48.160 --> 00:04:53.120
But apparently this wasn't enough, because Marvel&nbsp;
went out of their way to make the new improved&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:58.960
Professor Hulk seem even dorkier in "Avengers:&nbsp;
Endgame." When we meet Hulk five years after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:03.200
"The Snap," Bruce has figured out a way to&nbsp;
merge Banner's intellect with Hulk's body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:07.360
giving him the best of both worlds.&nbsp;
Yet he's still a walking Dad joke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.360 --> 00:05:13.120
as evidenced by the fact that, when approached by&nbsp;
a group of kids for an autograph, he does The Dab.

00:05:13.120 --> 00:05:17.680
"Hulk out! Dab!"

00:05:18.720 --> 00:05:24.640
The Dab, which became popular as a fad in 2015,&nbsp;
was already cringingly uncool when the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.640 --> 00:05:30.720
came out in 2018; the fact that he dropped&nbsp;
that move in the film's 2023 setting? Yeesh.

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.480
This one is a little forgivable with&nbsp;
the help of context. After Tony Stark&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:40.080
frees himself from the captivity of the&nbsp;
Ten Rings in the first "Iron Man" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.640
he tells Pepper Potts he wants to do two things.

00:05:42.640 --> 00:05:44.480
"I have been in captivity for three months,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.480 --> 00:05:49.120
there are two things that I want to do. I&nbsp;
want an American cheeseburger. And the other…"

00:05:49.120 --> 00:05:49.600
"That's enough."

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:53.280
When they arrive for a press&nbsp;
conference, Tony's driver, Happy Hogan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:56.720
dutifully trots around the the car so&nbsp;
Tony can retrieve another cheeseburger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.720 --> 00:06:01.120
from the Burger King bag. Really? You're&nbsp;
held in captivity, facing certain death,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.120 --> 00:06:05.920
only to cobble together a super suit from&nbsp;
spare weapons parts, engineer an escape plan,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.920 --> 00:06:11.920
survive a trip through the desert, and you hit&nbsp;
up the BK drive thru before facing the media?

00:06:11.920 --> 00:06:16.880
Of course, the main reason this scene was so&nbsp;
cringeworthy is that it was such a blatant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.880 --> 00:06:21.200
product plug. As the first film in the MCU,&nbsp;
of course, Marvel needed all the help it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.200 --> 00:06:26.560
could get to make sure their first film turned a&nbsp;
profit, but this was "Wayne's World" level stuff.

00:06:26.560 --> 00:06:29.360
"Contract or no, I will not bow to any sponsor."

00:06:31.680 --> 00:06:35.040
Ironically, Iron Man actor Robert&nbsp;
Downey Jr. has actually credited&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.040 --> 00:06:38.400
Burger King with helping him get&nbsp;
sober because their burgers are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:44.320
so terrible that he realized he had hit rock&nbsp;
bottom in 2003 after eating some of their food.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.320 --> 00:06:49.440
He told Empire the Whopper was so terrible he&nbsp;
ended up throwing all his drugs in the ocean.

00:06:49.440 --> 00:06:52.880
"It was such a disgusting&nbsp;
burger I ordered. I had that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.960
and this big soda, and I thought&nbsp;
something really bad was going to happen."

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:01.680
Jon Favreau, director of the first two Iron&nbsp;
Man movies, chose not to return for "Iron&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:01.680 --> 00:07:05.120
Man 3," which was written and directed&nbsp;
by Shane Black. Originally, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:10.320
Favreau had intended to make "Iron Man 3" - and&nbsp;
he had hoped to use the classic Iron Man villain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:14.960
The Mandarin. And Favreau had already alluded&nbsp;
to the baddy in "Iron Man" with the Ten Rings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.960 --> 00:07:18.800
organization, a nod to the character's&nbsp;
10 magic rings that give him power.

00:07:18.800 --> 00:07:22.560
But Black went a totally different&nbsp;
direction, using both The Ten Rings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:27.520
and The Mandarin as red herrings. In "Iron&nbsp;
Man 3," the terrorist known as The Mandarin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.520 --> 00:07:30.960
actually ends up being a British actor&nbsp;
named Trevor Slattery who was just hired&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:35.520
to play the part of a terrorist. It was all&nbsp;
a shell game to keep Tony Stark chasing his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:35.520 --> 00:07:39.440
tail while the real villain, Aldrich&nbsp;
Killian, moved behind the scenes.

00:07:39.440 --> 00:07:43.760
For many fans, it was unsatisfying and&nbsp;
even a slap in the face. Marvel had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:48.640
make amends by putting out the "All Hail the&nbsp;
King" short film as a promise to fans that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:48.640 --> 00:07:54.560
wait, there is a real Mandarin, so don't be too&nbsp;
mad! This finally paid off in "Shang Chi and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:59.360
Legend of the Ten Rings," but it took years&nbsp;
to get past this cringeworthy mistake.

00:08:00.320 --> 00:08:04.560
Speaking of cringeworthy blunders,&nbsp;
how about the time Star-Lord basically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.560 --> 00:08:08.640
killed half the living beings&nbsp;
in the entire universe because?

00:08:08.640 --> 00:08:11.840
You know what we're talking about. In&nbsp;
"Avengers: Infinity War," the Guardians&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:16.480
of the Galaxy team up with a group of Avengers&nbsp;
to try and take down Thanos. And their complex&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.480 --> 00:08:22.320
plan actually works perfectly - up until the&nbsp;
point where Star-Lord totally screws it all up.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:26.400
After learning that his girlfriend Gamora was&nbsp;
killed by Thanos in his successful bid to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:31.440
the Soul Stone, Star-Lord selfishly made it&nbsp;
all about his feelings, abandoning the plan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.440 --> 00:08:37.520
so he could get a couple ineffectual punches in.&nbsp;
Punches that Thanos probably didn't even feel.

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:42.640
But the rest of the universe felt it,&nbsp;
because Star-Lord selfishly going off script&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.640 --> 00:08:48.160
allowed Thanos to free himself, and ultimately&nbsp;
snap half of the universe out of existence.

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:52.640
Understandable? Sure. But that didn't stop&nbsp;
angry fans from taking out their displeasure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:57.360
on Star-Lord, even after - no thanks to&nbsp;
him - the Avengers later fixed everything.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.360 --> 00:09:01.840
So will fans come around when Star-Lord&nbsp;
returns in "Guardians of the Galaxy volume&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:08.000
3?" Or has this idiotic moment ruined&nbsp;
the character for good? Time will tell.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:13.760
And while we're on the topic of Star-Lord, we also&nbsp;
need to point out that he's really, really gross.

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:18.000
Sure, it makes sense. After all, Peter Quill&nbsp;
never knew his father, and his surrogate father&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.000 --> 00:09:23.600
was a blue-skinned alien pirate named Yondu who&nbsp;
kidnapped him, so it's understandable that Quill&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.600 --> 00:09:29.280
might develop certain anti-social behaviors,&nbsp;
like being disgustingly unhygienic at times.

00:09:29.280 --> 00:09:34.400
In fact, he seems oddly proud of it, or at the&nbsp;
very least uncaring when Gamora remarks on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.400 --> 00:09:39.280
general disarray of his ship, the Milano, in&nbsp;
the first "Guardians of the Galaxy" movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.280 --> 00:09:42.400
After the group discusses selling&nbsp;
the Orb — which contains the Power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:46.960
Stone — Star-Lord's green-hued love&nbsp;
interest informs him that his ship is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.960 --> 00:09:51.600
"filthy." Instead of taking offense,&nbsp;
as one might imagine, he leans into it

00:09:51.600 --> 00:09:57.600
"Oh, she has no idea. If I had a blacklight, this&nbsp;
place would look like a Jackson Pollock painting."

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:02.840
Pollock, of course, was a painter renowned for&nbsp;
making art by splashing and spraying paint willy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.840 --> 00:10:08.640
nilly everywhere. And since black lights&nbsp;
will cause certain bodily fluids to glow,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.640 --> 00:10:12.560
the kind we can't really&nbsp;
spell out any further… EW!

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:18.880
We accept and acknowledge that Captain America,&nbsp;
aka Steve Rogers, is a man from a different era.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.880 --> 00:10:24.240
Turned into a super-soldier during WWII, he spent&nbsp;
decades frozen in ice after crashing a bomber into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.240 --> 00:10:29.200
the ocean. He's technically a nonagenarian,&nbsp;
he admits he was in a barbershop quartet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:32.880
and he's definitely a bit naive at&nbsp;
times when it comes to the ladies.

00:10:32.880 --> 00:10:37.440
But while the Star-Spangled Avenger is as&nbsp;
wholesome and all-American as apple pie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:37.440 --> 00:10:40.400
there's no real reason to&nbsp;
think he's a square prude.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:44.720
So while the whole "Language" gag from&nbsp;
"Age of Ultron" was good for a quick laugh&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.720 --> 00:10:47.920
we're not sure it really makes that&nbsp;
much sense at the end of the day.

00:10:48.480 --> 00:10:50.080
"Fury, you son of a bitch."

00:10:50.080 --> 00:10:52.000
"Ooh, you kiss your mother with that mouth?!"

00:10:52.880 --> 00:10:57.600
Think about it: there's absolutely zero&nbsp;
doubt he'd have heard a lot worse while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.600 --> 00:11:03.360
fighting alongside enlisted men in WWII. He&nbsp;
led the legendary Howling Commandos in the MCU;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.360 --> 00:11:09.120
do you think they were howling "gosh," "darn,"&nbsp;
and "heck" when things didn't go their way?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.120 --> 00:11:14.320
Would he have stopped to admonish them as they&nbsp;
faced off against the German army and HYDRA? The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.320 --> 00:11:20.080
"Language" joke and its subsequent repetition&nbsp;
just come off a bit forced and try-hard.

